#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096307 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f247351-8c81-595e-bb30-26630159819c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Missing Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "97842ba7-b957-51da-9765-18cd94976e5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12333" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\dfrac{7x+6}{(x+2)^2} = \\dfrac{A}{x+2} -\\dfrac{8}{(x+2)^2}$,\n\nwhat is the value of $A$?" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+2)^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x + 6 &= A(x + 2) - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute some value for $x$ and solve for $A$.\n\nWe *can’t* choose $x=-2$, since this would eliminate $A$ from the equation. But we can choose any other value of $x$. So let’s choose a simple value, say, $x=0$.\n\nPlugging $x=0$ into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x + 6 &= A(x + 2) - 8 \\\\\n7(0) + 6 &= A(0 + 2) - 8 \\\\\n6 &= 2A - 8 \\\\\n14 &= 2A \\\\\nA &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096308 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0d98b82-24d2-5b2c-b596-0300778bd187" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Two-Term Partial Fraction Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "84d82f4d-b725-5779-a570-cbb3e0e9e429" :question/math-academy-id "e-12334" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\dfrac{7-4x}{(x+5)^2} = \\dfrac{A}{x+5} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+5)^2}$,\n\nfind the values of $A$ and $B$." :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+5)^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 - 4x &= A(x + 5) + B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to solve for $A$ and $B$, and both of these constants appear in the equation simultaneously. However, we can eliminate $A$ if we substitute $x=-5$.\n\nPlugging $x=-5$ into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 - 4x &= A(x + 5) + B \\\\\n7 - 4(-5) &= A(-5 + 5) + B \\\\\n27 &= A(0) + B \\\\\n27 &= 0 + B \\\\\n27 &= B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve for $A$. To do this, let’s start by substituting our value $B=27$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 - 4x &= A(x + 5) + 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can substitute some value for $x$ and solve for $A$.\n\nWe *can’t* choose $x=-5$, since this would eliminate $A$ from the equation. But we can choose any other value of $x$. So let’s choose a simple value, say, $x=0$.\n\nPlugging $x=0$ into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 - 4x &= A(x + 5) + 27 \\\\\n7 - 4(0) &= A(0 + 5) + 27 \\\\\n7 &= 5A + 27 \\\\\n- 20 &= 5A \\\\\nA &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=-4$ and $B=27$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096309 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c30dc350-d0c9-5a4d-87a0-4e2ebb1bb221" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Three-Term Partial Fraction Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "57242982-05a3-5104-9bf0-2c6f3350a781" :question/math-academy-id "e-12335" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\dfrac{3x^2-5x+8}{(x-3)^2(x+1)} = \\dfrac{A}{(x-3)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x-3)^2} + \\dfrac{C}{(x+1)}$,\nwhere $A$, $B$, and $C$ are real constants, calculate $A\\cdot B\\cdot C$." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the expression through by $(x-3)^2(x+1)$ to give\n\n$$\n3x^2-5x+8= A(x-3)(x+1)+B(x+1)+C(x-3)^2\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3$ eliminates the $A$ and $C$ terms and allows us to solve for $B$. We get\n\n$$\n3(3)^{2} - 5(3) + 8 = B \\cdot 4⟹B = 5\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-1$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C$. We get\n\n$$\n3(-1)^{2} - 5(-1) + 8 = C \\cdot (-4)^{2}⟹C = 1\n$$\n\nUnfortunately, there is no value of $x$ that we can substitute to eliminate both $B$ and $C$. However, using our results for $B$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n3x^2-5x+8= A(x-3)(x+1)+5(x+1)+1(x-3)^2\n$$\n\nand we can plug in any value of $x$ to solve for $A$. Let's choose the simplest number to plug in, $x=0$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8 &= A(0 - 3)(0 + 1) + 5(0 + 1) + C(0 - 3)^{2} \\\\\n8 &= -3A + 5 + 9 \\\\\n- 6 &= -3A \\\\\nA &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B\\cdot C=2\\cdot 5\\cdot 1=10\n$$\n\nand the partial fraction decomposition is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x^2-5x+8}{(x-3)^2(x+1)} = \\dfrac{2}{(x-3)} +\\dfrac{5}{(x-3)^2} + \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096310 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e9c4ef2-0491-5b42-8679-4fe7dfbd7b00" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Partial Fraction Decomposition With Higher Order Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "17bbb974-0c14-5201-a28a-825f4ea385bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12336" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$\\dfrac{1-2x}{(x+1)(x+2)^3}= \\dfrac{A}{x+1}+\\dfrac{B}{x+2} +\\dfrac{C}{(x+2)^2} + \\dfrac{D}{(x+2)^3}$,\n\nwhere $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$ are real constants, determine the value of $AB + CD$." :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)(x+2)^3$ gives\n\n$$\n1 - 2x = A(x+2)^3 + B(x+1)(x+2)^2 + C(x+1)(x+2) + D(x+1)\n$$\n\nLetting $x = -2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - 2(-2) &= A(-2 + 2)^{3} + B(-2 + 1)(-2 + 2)^{2} + C(-2 + 1)(-2 + 2) + D(-2 + 1) \\\\\n1 + 4 &= -D \\\\\nD &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x = -1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - 2(-1) &= A(-1 + 2)^{3} + B(-1 + 1)(-1 + 2)^{2} + C(-1 + 1)(-1 + 2) + D(-1 + 1) \\\\\n1 + 2 &= A \\\\\nA &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x = 0$ and using our values for $A$ and $D$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - 2(0) &= 3(0 + 2)^{3} + B(0 + 1)(0 + 2)^{2} + C(0 + 1)(0 + 2) + (-5)(0 + 1) \\\\\n1 - 0 &= 24 + 4B + 2C - 5 \\\\\n2C &= -4B - 18 \\\\\nC &= -2B - 9.(1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, letting $x = 1$ and using our values for $A$ and $D$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - 2(1) &= A(1 + 2)^{3} + B(1 + 1)(1 + 2)^{2} + C(1 + 1)(1 + 2) + D(1 + 1) \\\\\n1 - 2 &= 81 + 18B + 6C - 10 \\\\\n6C &= -18B - 72 \\\\\nC &= -3B - 12.(2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom $(1)$ and $(2)$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2B - 9 &= -3B - 12 \\\\\nB &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the value of $B$ into $(1)$ to get the value of $C{:}$\n\n$$\nC = -2(-3) - 9 = -3\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nAB + CD = 3(-3) + (-3)(-5) = 6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f247351-8c81-595e-bb30-26630159819c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Missing Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5911f92e-c722-5dd9-a026-cb757f6663fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266345} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d63ed0-db44-591a-8ec4-95304ca0941e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266346} {:answer/id #uuid "c91fc605-fea6-5b1c-9e21-8a2868061712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266347} {:answer/id #uuid "67db2cdf-4bcf-5ef5-955e-124c956cf029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266348} {:answer/id #uuid "0f7964b5-e1e4-581a-a353-fd990a8f2016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f7964b5-e1e4-581a-a353-fd990a8f2016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266349} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc62e31a-77c8-59cb-8644-112c17bf012c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f94d7586-f1d9-5b0b-8f86-74d808707b50" :question/math-academy-id "q-115105" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+8}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{5}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+2)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+8 & =5(x+2)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-2)+8 & =5(-2+2)+B \\\\ -2 & =0+B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e9c4ef2-0491-5b42-8679-4fe7dfbd7b00" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Partial Fraction Decomposition With Higher Order Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb4914a0-dcac-553b-a9c1-6e6174cafe91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb4914a0-dcac-553b-a9c1-6e6174cafe91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266402} :answer-field/id #uuid "cba7ad19-e9f0-5861-a329-d1b9a37ee778" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266401} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75a23c48-9a8f-566e-b0c3-fec4d39fbacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75a23c48-9a8f-566e-b0c3-fec4d39fbacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266404} :answer-field/id #uuid "8394f092-b34a-51bf-a410-97a0251f5a10" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266403} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d73c11d-74d9-53f9-8208-cc2720a8385c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d73c11d-74d9-53f9-8208-cc2720a8385c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266406} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ad10d37-524f-53f8-ba9d-3f87a9a80ee0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abf4a0bc-708c-5fd0-8c3f-6225acfa1d94" :question/math-academy-id "q-351243" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{7{x}^{2}-32x+42}{(x-2)^{3}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{7{x}^{2}-32x+42}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,=$ | {{field-1}} | $-$ | {{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x-2$ |  | $(x-2)^{2}$ |  | $(x-2)^{3}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-32x+42}{(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-2)^{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-32x+42=A(x-2)^{2}+B(x-2)+C.\n$$\n\nLetting $x=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(2)^{2}-32(2)+42 & =A(2-2)^{2}+B(2-2)+C \\\\ 6 & =C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=3$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(3)^{2}-32(3)+42 & =A(3-2)^{2}+B(3-2)+6 \\\\ 9 & =A+B+6 \\\\ B & =3-A.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, letting $x=0$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(0)^{2}-32(0)+42 & =A(0-2)^{2}+B(0-2)+6 \\\\ 42 & =4A-2B+6 \\\\ 36 & =4A-2B \\\\ B & =2A-18.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom $(1)$ and $(2)$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-A & =2A-18 \\\\ A & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the value of $A$ into $(1)$ to get the value of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =3-7 \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-32x+42}{(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{7}{x-2}-\\frac{4}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{6}{(x-2)^{3}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c30dc350-d0c9-5a4d-87a0-4e2ebb1bb221" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Three-Term Partial Fraction Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b013c49a-7235-534b-95cb-e6ec7aed0156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266409} {:answer/id #uuid "c1337fa2-6a85-5185-841d-9b62fa9933dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266410} {:answer/id #uuid "cebf97f7-73e5-579b-8173-2cc90559fbdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266411} {:answer/id #uuid "2949d997-b7df-5136-ab3e-d8d1234ea639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266412} {:answer/id #uuid "98ec2afa-d2d1-52d4-a6e4-7e098d49502a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98ec2afa-d2d1-52d4-a6e4-7e098d49502a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266413} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a5d8a44-e752-5b69-8903-594e4b8dff2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02be96e1-29ee-55e3-9149-ea13abece695" :question/math-academy-id "q-51141" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+6}{(x-1)^{2}(x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$, and $C$ are real constants, determine the value of $A\\cdot B\\cdot C.$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-1)^{2}(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+6=A(x-1)(x+1)+B(x+1)+C(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-1$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)+6 & =A(-1-1)(-1+1)+B(-1+1)+C(-1-1)^{2} \\\\ 4 & =4C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=1$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)+6 & =A(1-1)(1+1)+B(1+1)+C(1-1)^{2} \\\\ 8 & =2B \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, letting $x=0$ and using our values for $B$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)+6 & =A(0-1)(0+1)+4(0+1)+1(0-1)^{2} \\\\ 6 & =-A+4+1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nHence,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B\\cdot C=(-1)\\cdot 4\\cdot 1=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0d98b82-24d2-5b2c-b596-0300778bd187" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Two-Term Partial Fraction Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80b7ba36-3658-58ba-9dc5-6d4c0ab42388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095966} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f701c4-58b2-5c66-9f58-d825b1ab076e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095967} {:answer/id #uuid "243a13bd-e2ce-5e50-9da9-367011fade53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095968} {:answer/id #uuid "528bb06b-60ff-5b7e-b6ed-1177c007ab86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095969} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3717ad-b5b8-583e-8e6f-5f5106eaa105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "528bb06b-60ff-5b7e-b6ed-1177c007ab86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095969} :answer-field/id #uuid "b54909fb-1b81-5487-91fa-f334b65b8a47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095965}] :question/id #uuid "053ff6d8-5d73-5352-85bb-05c3c899eb26" :question/math-academy-id "q-73204" :question/problem "Given that\n$(x + 8)/((x - 2)^{2}) = \\frac{A}{x - 2} + (B)/((x - 2)^{2})$,\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants, what is the value of $A \\cdot B$?"}]}
